igma$ and $\\cosh\\rho = \\cos t/\\sin\\sigma$. Its edge $\\rho = 0$, reached as $t_R \\to \\pm\\infty$, is the pair of light rays through the middle of the wormhole at $t = 0$, which run on to the other boundary. The dilaton $\\phi = \\phi_h(1 + x\\arctan x)$, with $x = \\sinh\\rho\\cosh t_R$, changes with $t_R$, and the symmetry the wormhole keeps is the shift of the global time $t$."],"source":{"fields":["coords","parameters","metric_components","inverse_metric_components","kretschmann","domains"],"version":"425a571155e174e9"},"slices":[{"view":"cylinder","surface":0,"label":"$t = 0$","version":"9eb330d03de5c381","lines":[[[0.0,0.5],[0.2938,0.5]]],"points":[],"fills":[],"place":{"at":[0.2938,0.5],"anchor":"br","dx":0,"dy":-1.3}}]}]}}
